<meta type=article title="Structured-author interface fixture">
<author name="Jane Roe" orcid="0000-0000-0000-0000" affiliation="University A" +corresponding />
<author>
<name | Jane Roe>
<orcid | 0000-0000-0000-0000>
<affiliation | University A>
</author>
<author bogus="x" | Charles Darwin>
</meta>

<section | Body>

This fixture proves the `<author>` structured interface. The first two
`<author>` elements carry the *same* information in two surface forms:

1. The **kwarg form** — `name`, `orcid`, `affiliation` lift to child
   tags at the gate; `+corresponding` stays as a boolean kwarg on the
   canonical Layer 1 node. The kwarg form uses self-closing `/>` to
   disambiguate from a long-form opener (the same constraint
   `<table />` follows — `<author>` is now long-form-eligible, so a
   kwarg-only `<author …>` with no pipe and no `/` would be claimed
   as a long-form opener).
2. The **child-tag form** — the same fields authored directly as
   `<name>`, `<orcid>`, `<affiliation>` children. Long-form
   `<author>...</author>` is the natural shape here.

Both reduce to equivalent Layer 1 structures (the kwarg-form variant
also carries `+corresponding`, which is unique to it as the
corresponding-author marker).

The third `<author>` exercises the unknown-kwarg path: `bogus=x` is
not in the allowlist and is dropped with an informative diagnostic;
the pipe content "Charles Darwin" sits as text content of the
canonical `<author>` (the backward-compatible casual form — the pipe
in the opener disambiguates from long-form).

Note on indentation: nested long-form close tags (`</author>` inside
`<meta>`) must sit at column 0 of their line — the long-form close
tokenizer requires the `<` immediately after the line ending. This is
a known parser constraint (same one all long-form-eligible nested
tags follow); the fixture authors the body unindented to satisfy it.
